{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788300847,"nonce":"3orB3g7TXW6FaiY2cfsnSXFeyT8=","message":"THE ACCEPTANCE SUITE EXISTS, AND SO DO THE TWO DOC BULLETS. `accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py` plus `accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.md`, branch `spec/accept-bleed-triage`, commits `e6d9833` (the suite) and `cf7e2da` (the doc bullets). It covers this issue and `61794cd`; the same header comment is on both. **It has not been run against a bench — the orchestrator runs all in-game acceptance, and a worker never launches.** `--selftest` is green: **63 checks, exit 0**, over the raw envelopes at `accept/runs/s21-20260901/` and over deliberately broken copies.\n\n## Every acceptance bullet, marked\n\n| bullet | verdict |\n|---|---|\n| `digest` publishes `work_coverage` with enabled-and-capable per essential type against a stated floor; Doctor's floor is 2 | **COVERED** — phase 3. 3.3b (floor 2), 3.3c (`floor_on:\"available\"`), 3.3d (`floor_by`), and 3.4b asserts every non-Doctor floor is 1 |\n| the block distinguishes \"enabled but incapable\" from \"capable\", **proved with a real pawn** | **COVERED** — phase 4, with `dev:damage {mode:\"manipulation\", allow_bleeding:false}` (= `HealthUtility.DamageLimbsUntilIncapableOfManipulation`) then Doctor enabled on the same pawn. 4.4b-4.4d prove `enabled \u003e available` while `capable` is unchanged; 4.5d asserts `missing_capacity == \"Manipulation\"`; 4.5e that the pawn is not in `available_pawns` |\n| a roster change below the floor promotes the best remaining capable pawn, **journalled as an act** | **COVERED** — phase 5. The promotion at 5.3, and **5.4a-5.4d read the act out of the LEDGER** (`journal {types:[\"action\"]}`), not out of the verb's own stamp, and join `data.action.journal_seq` to the row's `seq`. 5.3h re-reads an INDEPENDENT digest, because a verb reporting what it intended is not the state afterwards |\n| when NO capable pawn remains the verb refuses explicitly | **COVERED** — phase 7, and **both gates in turn**: `too-few-candidates` (7.2, one candidate against a floor of two) then `no-candidate` (7.3, nobody left), each with `floor`/`floor_on`/`have`/`short_by`/`enabled_this_call`/`candidates_offered`/`capable`/`enabled`/`available`. 7.3g asserts both refusals reached the journal |\n| a downed colonist with a capable rescuer causes a forced `rescue`, journalled, not a priority change | **COVERED** — phase 6. 6.9 asserts `act`'s shape and that the rescuer it names is one it actually pathed; **6.10 SENDS `act` VERBATIM** and asserts `job_def == \"Rescue\"`, the doer it named, the bed, the target echoed back, and a journal seq |\n| no capable rescuer: the procedure says so explicitly | **COVERED** — 6.4a-6.4f, the `no-bed` case, asserted deliberately before a bed is staged |\n| with `61794cd` landed: an advance refused when travel exceeds the clock | **NOT BUILT, correctly** — `52606d1` left part 3 to `722c951`'s machinery and comment #2 says so. `triage` publishes both numbers and the verdict; 6.7a/6.7b assert the comparison (`margin_ticks == clock − best total`, and the verdict is that comparison's answer) so the refusal has everything to consume |\n| replay tick 231,968 and show the procedure reaches for `rescue` | **NOT DECIDABLE** — same reason as `61794cd`'s replay bullet: the transcript is output, not state, and there is no save. Reconstructed as the shape, said so in the suite |\n| `checklists/` item 7 shrinks to confirming the mod's read | **DONE** — `cf7e2da` |\n| `[[one-doctor-is-zero-doctors]]` keeps only the WHY | **DONE** — `cf7e2da` |\n| **`work_coverage` registered as a predicate section, `advance {until:{condition:{path:\"work_coverage.ok\"}}}` arms and halts** | **COVERED** — 7.1 arms it while coverage HOLDS (`true_when_armed:false`, `saw_false`, evaluated, exits on timeout — proving the path resolves and is polled) and 7.4 HALTS on it once the M1 end-state is staged (`reason:\"condition\"`, `halted_on.path`, `halted_on.kind`, `observed:false`, no `halted_seq`). Plus 0.10a-0.10c, the arm-time refusals |\n| the cap never truncates an under-covered row | **COVERED BY CONSTRUCTION, and said so** — see below |\n\n## SUITE versus YOUR MANUAL PASS\n\nComment #3 proved a great deal of this by hand. **Nothing below is claimed as new** — it is cited. What the suite adds is repeatability: a bullet you proved by hand cannot be re-proved on demand, and the suite is the deliverable.\n\n| already measured and banked by you (#3) | what the SUITE adds |\n|---|---|\n| `work_coverage` live: `ok:false`, `under:[\"Doctor\"]`, twelve rows, `have:1` vs `floor:2`, full diagnosis, eleven three-field FINE rows | 9.6a-9.6e re-assert your digest offline; phase 3 **constructs** the UNDER row rather than finding it (Doctor off everywhere, then on for one), and `keys_exactly` pins BOTH row shapes as SETS so a field added or dropped fails |\n| `Diplomat` picked up free with `floor:1` | 0.9c cross-checks the essential SET two ways — the list `work-cover`'s refusal names against `digest.total` — so a `visible` filter or sort that drops one fails |\n| `work-cover` promoted **Harrell** (`before:0, after:3, skill:3`), `coverage_after.ok` flipped, a following `digest` agreed, journalled at **seq 33** | 9.6s-9.6u re-assert your envelope `13`; phase 5 stages it live, and **5.4a-5.4d read the act out of the LEDGER and join `journal_seq` to the row's `seq`** rather than trusting the verb's stamp — plus 5.3h the independent digest |\n| `triage` through **four states of one casualty**, `no-rescuer`/`no-bed` → `in-time` with `act` | 9.6h-9.6p re-assert reads `16`/`18`/`20`; phase 6 stages the union as **three pawns each satisfying exactly ONE clause** (`Anesthetic` = downed-only, `Flu` = needs-tend-only, the Captain shape = bleeding), which one pawn through four states cannot show |\n| `act` = `{\"op\":\"rescue\",\"args\":{\"pawn\":359,\"target\":362}}`, both ids filled in | 6.9a-6.9f assert the shape and that the named rescuer is one triage actually pathed; **6.10 sends it verbatim and asserts `job_def:\"Rescue\"`** — with the clock stopped, which is the fix for the race #3 found |\n| the `cannot-rescue` refusal you got, and the ruling that it was the game being right | 9.6q/9.6r assert your envelope `21`; `pause()` and 6.10a exist BECAUSE of it, and the reason is in the function's header |\n| — | **`enabled_but_incapable` with a real handless pawn** (phase 4), which #3 lists as still owed |\n| — | **`too-few-candidates` AND `no-candidate`** (phase 7), which #3 lists as still owed |\n| — | **the `work_coverage` predicate section armed and halting** (7.1, 7.4), which #3 lists as still owed |\n| — | the two doc bullets, which #3 lists as still owed — done in `cf7e2da` |\n\n**ONLY your manual pass covers:** the four-states-of-one-casualty progression as such. The suite proves the same union with three single-clause pawns; your read is the better evidence that ONE pawn moves through them, and the suite cites it rather than restaging it.\n\n## The doc bullets\n\n`checklists/triggered.md` item 7 was a per-colonist scan: `pawn {sections:[\"work\"]}` once per colonist, N round-trips, a human comparing counts against a floor they had to remember. That arithmetic is now in every digest. The item is now read `work_coverage.ok`, branch on `under`, repair with `work-cover`, and read the `still_under` gate because each of the three wants a different follow-up. What it does NOT give up is the half the mod does not compute — no bill-relevant work type checked only on its likely patient — which stays, pointing at `[[who-will-actually-do-it]]`.\n\n`one-doctor-is-zero-doctors` drops its whole \"what to do\" section, which was three bullets the mod now does, and its header says so outright. What is left is the argument nobody can read off a digest: `Doctor.requireCapableColonist` is FALSE, the 2 comes from `Alert_NeedDoctor.Patients` having `!item.Downed` in its own predicate, and therefore the floor is on AVAILABILITY. Plus the two things that bite inside the emergency — every vanilla Doctor work-giver except `VisitSickPawn` requires Manipulation, and `tend` is drafted-only so the response to a downed colonist is `rescue`. `playbook/INDEX.md`'s row is re-tagged **graduated to CODE**.\n\n## THREE FINDINGS in the shipped source, reported not asserted\n\nThey print as `FINDING` lines and repeat in the summary. Deliberately not checks: the exit code answers \"were the acceptance bullets met\", and a suite that goes permanently red over a metadata string teaches the next session to ignore its own colour.\n\n**1. `work_coverage.order` describes an order the rows are not in.** It publishes `\"under-first, then natural-priority-desc\"`. `WorkCoverage.Section` makes ONE pass over the natural-priority-sorted list and appends under-rows **inline**:\n\n    foreach (var r in rows) {\n        if (r.Under) { under.Add(...); list.Add(d); continue; }   // \u003c- inline\n        if (list.Count - under.Count \u003e= RowCap) { dropped++; continue; }\n        list.Add(...);\n    }\n\nOn the banked s21 digest the only UNDER row (`Doctor`, naturalPriority 1300) sits at index **1**, behind `Firefighter` (1400). A caller trusting the string reads `rows[0]` as the worst problem; here `rows[0]` is fine. Check 9.6f re-asserts it from the banked envelope so it cannot be argued away. One-line fix either way: emit under-rows first, or change the string.\n\n**2. `enabled_but_incapable` is unreachable on a COVERED row.** It is built inside `if (r.Under)`, so a colony with two available doctors and a handless third is never told about the third. Arguably correct — the block is a diagnosis and there is no problem to diagnose — but \"you have a doctor who cannot tend\" is the class of thing this post-mortem exists about, and a colony one casualty away from it gets no warning. Not asserted either way; raised so it is a decision rather than an omission.\n\n**3. A clean `dry_run` reports \"the journal writer is closed\" when it is not.** `work-cover`'s emit guard is `(repaired.Count \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 !dryRun) || stillUnder.Count \u003e 0`, so a dry run that plans a clean repair emits nothing — correctly — and then reports it through `Stamp(0)`, whose provenance sentence is *\"NOT WRITTEN — the journal writer is closed, so this mutation has no journal line. Treat anything done in this session as unprovenanced.\"* Neither clause is true here. `NoStamp()` is the shape this case wants. Banked in envelope `12` and asserted at 9.6w. Side note: the same guard makes the `\"plan\"` step name unreachable except on a dry run that FAILED to fully repair.\n\nAlso noted, not a defect: `var after = dryRun ? WorkCoverage.Section(map) : WorkCoverage.Section(map);` — both arms identical. Harmless today, and it is the line `58794e4` is about.\n\n## On `58794e4`, and what the suite does about it\n\n**Neither value is asserted.** Asserting the current one makes this suite go red the day it is fixed; asserting the fixed one makes it red today. What IS asserted is the thing that is true either way and that the acceptance bullet actually needs: **the dry run mutated nothing**, proved from an INDEPENDENT digest (5.2a: the colony still has one available doctor) and from the journal (5.2b: no `work-cover` action row). A `note()` at 5.1g2 points at `58794e4` so a reader knows why the value is unasserted.\n\n## The cap, and why \"never truncates an under-covered row\" is proved by construction\n\n`WorkCoverage.RowCap` is **14**. A full-DLC bench publishes **12** essential types — the nine `requireCapableColonist` vanilla ones (Firefighter, Warden, Construction, Growing, Mining, PlantCutting, Crafting, Hauling, Cleaning; I counted them in `Data/Core/.../WorkTypes.xml`, and the \"twelve vanilla types\" in this issue, in DESIGN and in `WorkCoverage.cs`'s header is **nine**, with Childcare making ten), plus Childcare from Biotech, plus Doctor, plus whatever a mod adds (`Diplomat` on the s21 bench). **So the cap cannot fire and no live run can witness a truncation.**\n\nThe suite therefore asserts the property that survives that: 3.5a — every name in `under` is present in `rows` — plus 3.5b and 3.6a for the arithmetic. And **phase 9 re-derives `RowCap` out of `WorkCoverage.cs` (9.8b) and `HediffCap`, `DoctorFloor`, `PathCandidateCap`, the two `HealthCardUtility` thresholds and the `Bleedout` field list out of the source too**, so the day a mod set pushes the type count past 14 the argument is re-examined rather than quietly becoming false. That is the honest form of the bullet and it is what the `.md` says.\n\nNothing else in `WorkCoverage.cs`, `TriageVerbs.cs` or `work-cover` read wrong. The `Compute` snapshot of `FreeColonistsSpawned`, the `CalculateCapacityLevel` route around the lazy cache, the `Ranked` exclusions and the double `TakeToBedGate` call in `triage` (a second lookup, not a second reservation) are all correct as written.\n\n## The fixture the orchestrator must run\n\nEverything is done by the driver. The only manual steps are starting the bench and the in-game readout comparison, which the `.md` spells out. A `--quicktest` map is the BETTER bench here, because it has no beds and check 6.4 asserts the `no-bed` refusal live.\n\n    ./accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py --selftest    # first, offline\n    ./accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py               # then, on the bench\n\nIt pauses the game at the top of phase 0 and asserts the pause, for both reasons you measured: the fixture is a colonist on a ~7,000-tick clock, and `act` is a snapshot. It tops the roster to six with `dev:spawn-pawn`, assigns every subject a role once and prints them, and stages the union so each pawn satisfies exactly ONE clause — `Anesthetic` for downed-and-nothing-else (it is `isBad:false`, zeroes Consciousness, and downs WITHOUT wounding, which `mode:\"until-downed\"` cannot do), `Flu` for needs-tend-only, and phase 2's Captain shape for the bleeder. The bed is `dev:spawn-thing {def:\"Bed\", stuff:\"WoodLog\", pos:\"pawn:\u003cbleeder\u003e\", mode:\"direct\"}`, on the pawn WITH A CLOCK so `in-time` is an honest verdict.\n\n**It wrecks the colony and says so in its banner. Reload before running it twice.**\n\nThe `advance` escapes `722c951` introduces are injected in one module-level wrapper with a reason naming this file, and phase 7b is the only caller that omits them — 7.6a asserts an advance across this suite's own downed colonists does NOT come back as an ordinary completed advance, and 7.7a that the escape lets it through. That check asserts the INVARIANT, not `722c951`'s spelling, and if `722c951` has not merged it is the one that fails, honestly.","files":null}]}